@Article(time: 5) {
  @Intro(title: "Feeds") {
    Subscriptions are managed from the command line.

    ```sh
    rivulet add https://example.com/feed.xml --user alice
    rivulet add https://example.com/feed.xml --category news --user alice
    rivulet remove https://example.com/feed.xml --user alice
    rivulet list
    rivulet list --categories
    rivulet import feeds.opml --user alice
    rivulet export --file feeds.opml --user alice
    rivulet refresh
    rivulet status --json
    ```

    `add` needs an absolute URL and refuses a feed the account already has. Without `--category` the
    feed goes into the account's default category. `--user` can be left out when there is only one
    account. `add` stores the subscription without fetching it.

    `list` lists feeds; `--categories` lists categories and `--users` lists accounts, one listing
    per run. `import` and `export` read and write OPML.

    Feeds are fetched by the scheduler inside `rivulet serve`: every `POLLING_FREQUENCY` minutes it
    takes up to `BATCH_SIZE` feeds that are due and refreshes them, `WORKER_POOL_SIZE` at a time. A
    failing feed is reported and the rest continue; after `POLLING_PARSING_ERROR_LIMIT` consecutive
    errors a feed stops being polled. `rivulet refresh` runs one such pass by hand.

    Each feed refresh is one SQLite write transaction. Entries that disappear from a feed are kept,
    and an archived entry is not added back when the feed still lists it. Search uses SQLite's FTS5
    full-text index.
  }
}
